{"date":"2026-09-09","heading":"September 9, 2026","editions":[{"edition":"morning","byline":"Dawn patrol","text":"The Eastern Screech-Owl held the dark from midnight until dawn, registering eight detections across the pre-sunrise hours. As the light broke at 07:10, the American Crow took over, producing a sudden burst of thirty-three calls in the first hour. A single Red-shouldered Hawk passed through the same window, adding a brief, high-frequency note to the crow’s racket.\n\nBy mid-morning, the station has recorded 108 detections and seven species, far outpacing the recent average of ten. The Carolina Wren dominates the count with forty-nine calls, becoming the clear vocal leader. This activity level is a stark contrast to yesterday, which closed with seventy-nine total detections and only five species identified.\n\nNo visual sightings have been logged today. The Eastern Towhee contributed twelve detections, peaking in the late morning, while the Blue Jay added two brief notes to the mix.","time":"10:30"},{"edition":"afternoon","byline":"Field notebook","text":"The Ruby-throated Hummingbird provided the station’s first visual record of the day, hovering near the feeder at 11:44. This was followed by a Tufted Titmouse at 12:13, which perched on the wire facing right, and a Carolina Wren that visited the feeder at 15:21. These three birds constitute the only confident visual sightings logged so far.\n\nToday’s count of ninety-nine detections is significantly higher than the recent average of twenty-six for this time of day. The Carolina Wren remains the most frequent vocalizer with fifty-seven calls, while the American Crow contributed thirty-seven detections. The Eastern Towhee added twenty-six calls to the afternoon tally.\n\nThe weather remains calm with a high of 28.7 C and light winds. A Red-shouldered Hawk passed through at midday, adding five detections to the afternoon record.","time":"15:30"},{"edition":"evening","byline":"Closing entry","text":"The evening brought a marked shift in activity, with twelve of the day's twenty-two visits occurring after 6:30 PM. This final hour was the busiest of the day, dominated by a Northern Mockingbird that appeared three times and a Blue Jay that registered two probable visits alongside a confident Carolina Chickadee. The station’s total of twenty-two visits stands in stark contrast to recent days, which averaged zero visual records up to this hour.\n\nWhile earlier editions noted the vocal dominance of the Carolina Wren and American Crow, the late evening saw the Blue Jay and Red-bellied Woodpecker emerge as the primary audibly active species, with twelve detections each in the final hour. The Northern Mockingbird, previously absent from the visual log, provided the last clear sighting of the day, perched on a wire at 19:27 just before sunset. This late surge closes out a day that significantly outpaced the recent average of forty-two detections.","time":"20:30"},{"edition":"recap","byline":"Yesterday in review","text":"The station closed with its busiest activity in the past week, recording 23 feeder visits and 11 species, a sharp contrast to the five preceding days which averaged zero visual sightings. The high count aligns with the installation of a nectar feeder and a tube seed feeder on September 7. These three species, along with the Carolina Chickadee and Blue Jay, were not recorded during the previous week, marking a significant expansion in the visual log.\n\nThe Ruby-throated Hummingbird made the first confident visual appearance of the day, appearing 274 minutes after sunrise. By the end of the day, the Northern Mockingbird provided the last clear sighting, perched on a wire 20 minutes before sunset.\n\nThe Blue Jay and Red-bellied Woodpecker led the late-day detections, adding to the day's total of 238 vocal records. This surge in both visual and audio data stands out against the recent quiet period, where detection averages hovered around 42.","time":"06:41"}],"sun